WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.269
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Proficiency.

2
00:00:05.569 --> 00:00:08.688
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.988 --> 00:00:16.668
Unit ten: Colonialism and Its Legacies. In this unit, you will analyse the lasting effects of colonial history.

4
00:00:17.683 --> 00:00:19.091
colonial legacy.

5
00:00:19.791 --> 00:00:25.018
colonial legacy means effects of colonial rule that continue after independence.

6
00:00:25.318 --> 00:00:29.454
Borders drawn by colonial powers are part of the colonial legacy.

7
00:00:30.154 --> 00:00:33.619
Your turn. Say: colonial legacy.

8
00:00:36.319 --> 00:00:37.807
colonial legacy.

9
00:00:38.817 --> 00:00:40.236
decolonisation.

10
00:00:40.936 --> 00:00:47.649
decolonisation means the process of colonies gaining independence; also undoing colonial thinking.

11
00:00:47.949 --> 00:00:51.551
Decolonisation transformed the map of Asia and Africa.

12
00:00:52.251 --> 00:00:55.402
Your turn. Say: decolonisation.

13
00:00:58.102 --> 00:00:59.382
decolonisation.

14
00:01:00.383 --> 00:01:01.477
protectorate.

15
00:01:02.177 --> 00:01:06.986
protectorate means a territory controlled and 'protected' by a stronger state.

16
00:01:07.286 --> 00:01:12.432
Cambodia became a French protectorate in 1863.

17
00:01:13.132 --> 00:01:16.365
Your turn. Say: protectorate.

18
00:01:19.065 --> 00:01:20.043
protectorate.

19
00:01:21.050 --> 00:01:22.457
indentured labour.

20
00:01:23.157 --> 00:01:29.952
indentured labour means work under a contract that bound workers for years, often in harsh conditions.

21
00:01:30.252 --> 00:01:34.504
Indentured labour moved millions across the British Empire.

22
00:01:35.204 --> 00:01:38.344
Your turn. Say: indentured labour.

23
00:01:41.044 --> 00:01:42.312
indentured labour.

24
00:01:43.317 --> 00:01:45.038
cultural imperialism.

25
00:01:45.738 --> 00:01:50.802
cultural imperialism means imposing one culture's values on another.

26
00:01:51.102 --> 00:01:55.168
Critics see cultural imperialism in global media.

27
00:01:55.868 --> 00:01:59.055
Your turn. Say: cultural imperialism.

28
00:02:01.755 --> 00:02:03.336
cultural imperialism.

29
00:02:04.350 --> 00:02:05.653
reparations.

30
00:02:06.353 --> 00:02:09.467
reparations means compensation for past wrongs.

31
00:02:09.767 --> 00:02:13.601
Debates about reparations continue in many countries.

32
00:02:14.301 --> 00:02:17.499
Your turn. Say: reparations.

33
00:02:20.199 --> 00:02:21.328
reparations.

34
00:02:22.317 --> 00:02:24.072
neo-colonialism.

35
00:02:24.772 --> 00:02:30.638
neo-colonialism means indirect control of poorer countries through economic power.

36
00:02:30.938 --> 00:02:34.935
Some describe unfair trade deals as neo-colonialism.

37
00:02:35.635 --> 00:02:39.111
Your turn. Say: neo-colonialism.

38
00:02:41.811 --> 00:02:43.172
neo-colonialism.

39
00:02:44.183 --> 00:02:46.125
post-colonial identity.

40
00:02:46.825 --> 00:02:53.225
post-colonial identity means how formerly colonised people define themselves after independence.

41
00:02:53.525 --> 00:02:56.813
Language choices shape post-colonial identity.

42
00:02:57.513 --> 00:03:01.628
Your turn. Say: post-colonial identity.

43
00:03:04.328 --> 00:03:06.026
post-colonial identity.

44
00:03:07.017 --> 00:03:09.109
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:03:09.409 --> 00:03:15.034
Ms. Sarah, Cambodia was a French protectorate for ninety years. Does that still matter?

46
00:03:16.350 --> 00:03:21.870
It would be hard to argue that it doesn't. Colonial legacies tend to persist in subtle ways.

47
00:03:23.183 --> 00:03:27.319
Like the French words in Khmer, or the old administrative buildings?

48
00:03:28.617 --> 00:03:33.997
Those, certainly. Some historians would also point to borders and legal systems.

49
00:03:35.283 --> 00:03:37.898
And what about neo-colonialism today?

50
00:03:39.183 --> 00:03:45.748
That's more contested. Economic dependence may resemble it, though the analogy shouldn't be pushed too far.

51
00:03:47.050 --> 00:03:50.141
Should former colonial powers pay reparations?

52
00:03:51.450 --> 00:03:56.491
There's a strong moral case, I'd suggest, though the practicalities are formidable.

53
00:03:57.783 --> 00:04:01.327
Grammar focus: Modality and nuance.

54
00:04:01.627 --> 00:04:04.892
Modality allows nuanced, careful opinions.

55
00:04:05.192 --> 00:04:13.824
It would be hard to argue that it doesn't matter. Dependence may resemble neo-colonialism, though the analogy shouldn't be pushed too far.

56
00:04:14.124 --> 00:04:16.286
There's a strong case, I'd suggest.

57
00:04:16.586 --> 00:04:23.392
On sensitive historical topics, careful modality allows strong opinions without overstatement.

58
00:04:24.383 --> 00:04:28.046
Now read and listen. The Long Shadow of Empire.

59
00:04:28.346 --> 00:04:33.910
Between the sixteenth and twentieth centuries, European powers colonised much of the world.

60
00:04:35.217 --> 00:04:46.121
In Southeast Asia, Cambodia became a French protectorate in 1863 and gained independence in 1953.

61
00:04:47.417 --> 00:04:54.037
Decolonisation transformed global politics, creating dozens of new nations within a few decades.

62
00:04:55.350 --> 00:05:01.831
Yet colonial legacies may persist long after independence, from borders to languages and institutions.

63
00:05:03.117 --> 00:05:08.982
Systems of indentured labour moved millions of workers across empires, reshaping societies.

64
00:05:10.283 --> 00:05:17.507
Some scholars argue that economic dependence today resembles neo-colonialism, though others dispute the comparison.

65
00:05:18.817 --> 00:05:24.868
Debates about reparations and the return of cultural artefacts continue across the world.

66
00:05:26.183 --> 00:05:33.001
Post-colonial identity, it would seem, involves both reclaiming the past and redefining the future.

67
00:05:34.317 --> 00:05:37.213
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:05:37.513 --> 00:05:40.046
Compensation for past wrongs is blank.

69
00:05:40.346 --> 00:05:45.126
A, reparations. B, protectorate. C, decolonisation.

70
00:05:49.117 --> 00:05:51.999
The answer is A: reparations.

71
00:05:53.483 --> 00:05:56.040
Which sentence expresses the most nuance?

72
00:05:56.340 --> 00:06:02.908
A, It matters.. B, It would be hard to argue that it doesn't matter.. C, It doesn't matter..

73
00:06:06.917 --> 00:06:11.296
The answer is B: It would be hard to argue that it doesn't matter..

74
00:06:12.783 --> 00:06:15.270
The comparison blank be pushed too far.

75
00:06:15.570 --> 00:06:19.526
A, shouldn't. B, mustn't to. C, won't to.

76
00:06:23.517 --> 00:06:25.551
The answer is A: shouldn't.

77
00:06:27.050 --> 00:06:30.048
Indirect control through economic power is blank.

78
00:06:30.348 --> 00:06:36.811
A, neo-colonialism. B, indentured labour. C, colonial legacy.

79
00:06:40.817 --> 00:06:44.337
The answer is A: neo-colonialism.

80
00:06:45.850 --> 00:06:49.765
According to the text, when did Cambodia gain independence?

81
00:06:50.065 --> 00:06:59.349
A, 1863. B, 1953. C, 1979.

82
00:07:03.350 --> 00:07:06.673
The answer is B: 1953.

83
00:07:08.183 --> 00:07:15.236
Now it's your turn. Part one: How does the colonial past still shape the present in Cambodia or elsewhere?

84
00:07:20.536 --> 00:07:27.481
Part two: Use careful modality: give two examples and qualify one claim.

85
00:07:33.781 --> 00:07:45.620
It would be hard to deny some lasting influence. French loanwords and certain legal structures are obvious examples. One might also point to economic patterns, though that link shouldn't be overstated.

86
00:07:45.920 --> 00:07:48.024
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:07:49.017 --> 00:07:54.304
Great work today! You learned eight new words and modality and nuance.

88
00:07:54.604 --> 00:07:59.730
Today's value is love of country. Love and take care of Cambodia.

89
00:08:00.030 --> 00:08:02.659
See you in Unit eleven. Goodbye!

90
00:08:02.959 --> 00:08:05.890
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

